+# Claude Code execution
+
+Read this reference before launching, steering, waiting for, or collecting pstack workers. Resolve bundled files from the actual installed skill path. This reference binds the upstream workflow to Claude Code. The calling workflow still owns its scope, roles, retries, dropouts, model-diversity rules, fresh-worker rules, and parent review.
+
+## Resolve roles and models
+
+Run the sibling setup helper by absolute path:
+
+```sh
+node "<skills>/setup-pstack/scripts/models.mjs" resolve --role "<role>" --kind general-purpose
+```
+
+The worker kinds are `general-purpose`, `poteto-agent`, `comment-sicko`, and `read-only`. Use `poteto-agent` inside playbook steps, `comment-sicko` for no-comments, and `general-purpose` for routed investigation, candidate, review, and synthesis seats. Use `read-only` where the caller requires `readonly: true`, including How, Arena judges, and maintain-verification source-wave children. Recall investigators and Reflect use `general-purpose` and keep their MCP access. Pass the relevant skill, template, rubric, and project instructions in every brief.
+
+The helper reads `${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-~/.claude}/pstack-cc/models.json`. Explicit entries preserve their executor, exact model, and reasoning effort. Scalar roles stay scalar. `arena runners`, `architect runners`, and `interrogate reviewers` are ordered panels. Run every seat, including duplicate selections and parent aliases. `arena cross-judge pool` is an ordered selection pool, so it launches one eligible judge rather than the whole list. `swarm workers` is scalar unless an explicit race assigns models per arm.
+
+A missing role returns native `inherit-parent` entries with source `unconfigured`, without setup or generated definitions. Scalars yield one entry and panels or pools yield three initial entries. Their `agentType` uses the static inherited plugin definition. Disclose the native-parent resolution. Setup proposals remain unresolved until accepted. Saved exact choices remain authoritative. There is no hidden family default or default-pool retry.
+
+For a real model, use the caller's explicit task effort when given. Otherwise adjust its saved starting effort through poteto-mode's Subagents guidance. Keep the exact model ID and send effort and speed as separate supported controls. Report unavailable independent speed controls. Do not append effort or `-fast` to model IDs. Keep a rejected-model record per executor and never resubmit a rejected ID. Follow the caller's fallback and dropout rules, with disclosure. An unavailable executor is a capability gap, not a rejected model.
+
+`auto` and `inherit-parent` bind to the actual native Claude parent, regardless of the other workers' executors. Their returned `agentType` selects the plugin's inherited base definition. Omit invocation model and effort overrides. Never translate a parent alias to Codex or to an assumed Claude alias. Inspect host configuration if inheritance is overridden. Report unresolved or substituted effective settings rather than claiming the requested model ran.
+
+Validate required different-model or different-family relationships before the affected step. A repeated seat is still a seat, but it does not establish diversity. Use eligible exact choices already resolved by that workflow in their existing order, or an explicitly supplied task counterpart, for a required non-author verifier or final audit. Do not scan unrelated roles or add a verifier role or ranking. Parent aliases and unknown families cannot establish a different-family relationship. Families follow Arena's Phase A. Fable is its own family, and Sonnet and Opus belong to the same Claude family. A configuration that cannot satisfy a required gate remains a configuration gap. Arena alone permits its disclosed different-vendor judge skip and unknown-parent behavior. Its judge compares vendor, and Fable and the other Claude models are one vendor. Orchestrate's tool-error retry needs a different model, not necessarily another family.
+
+## Native Claude workers
+
+For `executor: "claude-code"`, use the exposed native `Agent` or `Task` delegation tool with the returned `agentType` as `subagent_type`, following its actual schema. Setup owns generated definitions for each exact Claude model and worker kind. Saved exact selections omit invocation model overrides. Pass supported per-invocation `effort` for an exact model. Inherited aliases omit both overrides. Preserve the returned identity and inspect actual settings when the host exposes them.
+
+The [Claude subagent reference](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents) documents exact model IDs in definitions, invocation effort, completion, and stateful messaging. Host policy can substitute models. Requested settings are not observed settings.
+
+The brief names the goal, owned files or slice, forbidden changes, assigned checkout and branch, resolved starting SHA, exact verification, output contract, and absolute skill and artifact paths. Writers are not alone in the repository and must accommodate others' changes. A readonly caller uses kind `read-only` and an explicit "do not edit files" brief. Its native definition disallows `Write`, `Edit`, and `NotebookEdit`, while retaining Bash and the selected model. Check `git status --short` before dispatch and after completion for unexpected changes. This restricts file tools, not OS writes through Bash or interpreters. Other no-edits tasks keep their caller's kind. Do not substitute Explore, since it can change the selected model and role body.
+
+Pass the absolute `poteto-mode/SKILL.md` path to every poteto-agent. The worker uses its complete supplied body, including the Principles index, before work. If that body is missing or incomplete, its first action is an actual full Read of the file. Require complete bodies or reads of applied principle leaves as upstream specifies. Inspect the actual input, worker transcript, and actions when chain-following matters. Supplied content alone does not prove compliance.
+
+Use the exposed native `Agent` or `Task` schema's asynchronous controls. Set `run_in_background: true` when that field is supported, including a steer or resume, as upstream requires. Follow the actual host's asynchronous mechanism when the field differs instead of assuming a background default. Launch independent seats without waiting between them and keep every agent ID. Native completion delivers a result. Use the exposed result or task retrieval tool when needed. An acknowledgement or completed status without its report is not a result. A concurrency limit queues work, not permission to omit required seats.
+
+## Codex workers
+
+For `executor: "codex"`, write the consolidated task to a local prompt file. The workflow's main conversation launches and owns the native Bash task. Run the foreground runner in background Bash with `run_in_background: true` and `timeout: 7200000` when the actual schema supports that maximum. Current native Bash defaults background tasks to 600000 ms and caps them at 7200000 ms. Honor the exposed host limit if it differs and report it. Do not shell-background the command with `&`, detach it, or add a relay agent. The foreground command remains active until the actual Codex turn terminates or the host stops it, allowing the host to deliver its completion event.
+
+Pass this launch request to native Bash:
+
+```json
+{
+  "command": "node \"<poteto-mode>/scripts/run-codex.mjs\" run --cwd \"/absolute/assigned-worktree\" --run-dir \"/absolute/unique-local-run-directory\" --model \"<selected-exact-model>\" --effort \"<supported-task-effort>\" --instruction \"/absolute/applicable/AGENTS.md\" --skill \"/absolute/relevant/SKILL.md\" --role \"<plugin>/agents/poteto-agent.md\" --write --prompt-file \"/absolute/consolidated-task.txt\"",
+  "run_in_background": true,
+  "timeout": 7200000
+}
+```
+
+Use `--role` for the selected original role body. `general-purpose` seats pass their body plus the workflow templates. Repeat `--instruction` for project rules, source references, rubrics, and templates, and `--skill` for selected skill bodies. Use `--write` only for authorized `workspace-write` tasks. Without it, the runner defaults to `read-only`. An explicit `--sandbox` must match the authorized task scope. Never silently broaden it after a denial. The runner starts each Codex thread with `on-request` approval and the `auto_review` reviewer, so a command that the sandbox blocks can request reviewed escalation. The reviewer decides whether it runs.
+
+Do not infer that browser, local-server, or network work is unavailable from the sandbox setting alone. Assign the authorized work to the selected Codex worker with the [macOS escalation guidance](worker-context.md). Before reducing its scope or applying the workflow's fallback rules, inspect the actual command, error, and approval outcome. A sandbox restriction, an explicit denial, a missing tool, and an unsupported client approval request are different outcomes. The runner cannot answer approvals sent back to its client. Report that capability gap when it occurs. Do not switch executors to bypass a denied action.
+
+Pass the absolute poteto-mode skill with `--skill` for a poteto-agent. Pass context paths to the runner instead of reading and copying their bodies into the brief. The runner reads the files and includes their complete bodies in its task input. The worker applies the [receiving-worker context rules](worker-context.md). Required reads of bodies absent from the input still need transcript evidence.
+
+Each independent seat and new round gets a unique run directory and fresh Codex thread. Use `--resume EXACT_THREAD_ID` only under poteto-mode's stateful-reuse exception. Never resume the latest thread or infer identity from a directory name. Setup prepares the official server and makes it available on inherited PATH. The runner calls `codex-app-server --session-source app-server`. Absolute-path flags override one invocation for diagnostics and are not saved. If runtime preparation is incomplete, use [setup prerequisites](../../setup-pstack/references/prerequisites.md). There is no umbrella CLI fallback.
+
+Workers share the existing Codex account, configuration, and persistent rollout/state storage through inherited `CODEX_HOME`. Fresh roots use the server's `app-server` session source without a `threadSource` tag. They remain persistent and can resume by exact thread ID. Existing threads retain their original source on resume and are not migrated or hidden retroactively. Ordinary Desktop lists filter session sources, but a successful help probe alone does not verify screen behavior or descendant visibility. Claude tools, MCP servers, conversation history, and permissions do not automatically become Codex tools or state. Verify the external executor has the capabilities needed for its assigned task.
+
+Track the native Bash task ID and collect its terminal outcome through the host's task-result controls. Read `<run-dir>/result.json` and `<run-dir>/final.txt` for the retained receipt and final answer. Bash stdout can truncate. Inspect the actual exit outcome, exact Codex thread identity, terminal status, result, requested and observed settings, and evidence paths. A final-answer item before a later failed terminal event is not success. Read the retained prompt, request, progress, diagnostics, and result when investigating failure. Review the worker's files and verification evidence under the calling workflow's rules.
+
+The outbound runner automatically includes the shared [receiving-worker execution context](worker-context.md) in its task payload. It keeps the launching configuration authority and distinguishes native receiving tools from parent launch recipes. The task still supplies its original role, selected model and effort, scope, and permissions.
+
+## Antigravity workers
+
+For `executor: "antigravity"`, preserve the returned exact `model` and separate `reasoningEffort`. Read [Antigravity execution](agy-execution.md) for the official agy runner, scoped session permissions, complete context, terminal receipts, cumulative usage, and exact continuation. Use this host's asynchronous command controls and collect every terminal receipt. Antigravity entries have no native agent type. Parent aliases stay native. Use known exact-model lineage for diversity. Custom or virtual routes with unknown lineage remain unknown. Preserve the calling workflow's selected-pool, cardinality, fresh-worker, required capability, and family gates.
+
+## Worktrees and state transfer
+
+Native `isolation: "worktree"` is available when its base and copy behavior meet the workflow. Otherwise create an explicit Git worktree at the resolved base commit before dispatch. Every independent writer owns a branch and checkout. A Cursor cloud-agent instruction maps to an exclusive local worktree, not a new machine. For a task that needs a remote host, use a real configured remote environment and identify it.
+
+Workers share the machine, repository metadata, credentials, and devices. Assign separate ports, cache and data directories, temporary paths, and output files. Serialize a resource that cannot be separated. The parent prepares worktrees and reviews accepted artifacts before integration. Retain unintegrated work and diagnostic evidence.
+
+Conversation history is not filesystem state. When a review includes uncommitted changes, record the source checkout and exact HEAD. Create all receiving worktrees at that HEAD. Capture scoped tracked final contents with `git diff --binary HEAD -- <scope>`. Inventory and copy scoped untracked files separately, preserving bytes, file types, symlink targets, and executable modes. Include ignored input only when the task needs it. Apply that same captured input to each receiver and compare its files, modes, deletions, and untracked inventory with the source before launch. Recapture if the source changes during the transfer.
+
+A fresh worker can receive a stopped writer's checkout or a copied committed and uncommitted state. A history fork copies no process. Reuse a worker only when its costly checkout state, uncommitted changes, or live process meets poteto-mode's exception. A role outlives an individual worker. New rounds otherwise get a fresh consolidated brief with every later directive and prior report.
+
+## Steering, results, and host features
+
+Use the exposed native messaging control, such as `SendMessage`, for a running Claude worker's steering or a permitted stateful resume by exact agent ID. Keep stop orders distinct from new briefs. Stop a Codex Bash task through the host's actual task-stop control and collect the resulting receipt. Do not equate interrupt delivery with observed process death. Retain exact thread identity for a later permitted continuation.
+
+Wait for all required terminal results before synthesis. Account for every seat and apply only the calling workflow's failure or dropout rule. The parent reviews diffs and reruns load-bearing claims. Never accept an author summary as a non-author verdict.
+
+Read [Claude Code wakeups](claude-wakeups.md) for repeated watchers and schedules, and [Claude history](../../recall/references/claude-history.md) for transcripts. Use native TodoWrite or task tools when offered, otherwise maintain the workflow's list in its local tracker. Inspect the live schemas rather than assuming a Project store or a PR-event subscription exists.
+
+For another pstack skill, use its complete supplied body or Read its installed `SKILL.md`, then follow it. Most pstack skills are explicit-only, so the Skill tool may refuse them. User invocation is `/pstack-cc:<name>`. User-authored skills live in `.claude/skills/` or `${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-~/.claude}/skills/`. Follow [skill authoring](skill-authoring.md) for a create-skill reference. Use native AskUserQuestion when available, or a plain question when it is absent.
+
+Claude Code reads `CLAUDE.md` and supports `AGENTS.md` under its [project instruction rules](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory#agentsmd). Do not rewrite unrelated instruction files or global settings to force a match. Pass applicable project-rule paths explicitly for external Codex tasks. Bugbot comments remain ordinary forge review threads. Use the calling workflow's `gh` or Origin commands. Cursor-specific configuration is not an instruction to install Cursor.
